    "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant Using a Laplace Expansion Across a Row",
    "problem": "Compute the following determinant using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd row:\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Using cofactor expansion along the third row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =4\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-2\\cdot 16=-32.\n$$",
